In clinical trials and were associated with reduced antitumor activity\(^{54,55}\). In addition to the CARs themselves, modified cells may also present vector-encoded immunogenic epitopes\(^{4}\). The fish-derived SB transposase is probably immunogenic in humans, but the short half-life of hsSB essentially reduces the immunogenic potential of engineered cells to that of the genetic cargo alone. From a regulatory point of view, the low immunogenic potential and the absence of transposase activity after \( 72\,\mathrm{h} \) promote early release of the cell product\(^{19}\). Thus, SBprotAct enables the generation of genetically stable and transposase-free CAR T cells within a time window of three or even fewer days.

Therefore, we anticipate that SBprotAct will be a valuable tool for CAR T cell and other gene therapies and will have a substantial effect on their cost and accessibility. The hsSB transposase is compatible with large-scale and automated production, and the quality and regulatory requirements are similar to those of other therapeutic proteins, such as monoclonal antibodies. Thus, the manufacturing and implementation of hsSB into clinical trials should be straightforward. Moreover, hsSB's profound stability and the ability to freeze and thaw the protein without compromising its activity allow shipping to point-of-care locations. Because SBprotAct contains no infectious agents, it can be handled at the lowest biosafety
